    {
      "text": "something we say really often is that the models are grown, not designed, right?",
      "timestamp_sec": 149,
      "why": "the framing the whole talk rests on"
    },
    {
      "text": "what contains them is us, right? The harness we put them in and the way we prompt them is basically like a function of our understanding of Claude",
      "timestamp_sec": 149,
      "why": "states the core thesis that the harness, not the model, is the bottleneck"
    },
    {
      "text": "We call this capability overhang, right? Claude gets smarter in spiky ways.",
      "timestamp_sec": 239,
      "why": "names the central concept in one line"
    },
    {
      "text": "if you give it arms, like you give it the bash tool and ways to work with the environment, it can build and search its own context. And that's sort of like the insight that led to Claude Code",
      "timestamp_sec": 314,
      "why": "insider account of the design decision behind Claude Code, and a position against giant context windows"
    },
    {
      "text": "we recently removed 80% of the system prompt for Claude code",
      "timestamp_sec": 347,
      "why": "concrete number about production prompting practice"
    },
    {
      "text": "The examples tend to constrain it cuz it's actually more imaginative than the examples we give it.",
      "timestamp_sec": 386,
      "why": "reverses a widely held few-shot prompting convention"
    },
    {
      "text": "we tried to give it context and not just constraints. We're really trying to avoid being like, \"Do not do this.\"",
      "timestamp_sec": 386,
      "why": "actionable prompting guidance that contradicts older negative-instruction habits"
    },
    {
      "text": "I really like to emphasize that this is closer to a biology than a physics, right? It's still very empirical, very organic.",
      "timestamp_sec": 499,
      "why": "crisp epistemic stance on how to reason about model behavior"
    },
    {
      "text": "the plan and prompt and spec that I have in my mind is the map, right? But the territory is the actual code base, the real world, the constraints that Claude needs to navigate",
      "timestamp_sec": 538,
      "why": "sets up the talk's second half"
    },
    {
      "text": "whenever Claude runs into something in the territory that's not in the map, I call that an unknown",
      "timestamp_sec": 538,
      "why": "defines the unit of analysis for his prompting workflow"
    },
    {
      "text": "Fable is one of the first models where I felt that like I really have to figure out my unknowns because if not, it's going to traverse such a large area that like it's going to run into a lot of them.",
      "timestamp_sec": 577,
      "why": "explains why more capable models raise, not lower, the specification burden"
    },
    {
      "text": "I like to do what I call a blind spot pass.",
      "timestamp_sec": 646,
      "why": "names the most reusable technique in the talk"
    },
    {
      "text": "in many ways, the model knows more about you know, almost everything than I do. I just need to get it out of it.",
      "timestamp_sec": 685,
      "why": "reframes the user's job as elicitation rather than instruction"
    },
    {
      "text": "One of the best ways to give Claude a map is to give it another map",
      "timestamp_sec": 757,
      "why": "compact argument for reference implementations over written specs"
    },
    {
      "text": "It was like the things that would have taken me weeks, I could do in hours",
      "timestamp_sec": 903,
      "why": "his personal magnitude estimate on a real legacy codebase"
    },
    {
      "text": "One of my favorite parts of Anthropic is that we believe that tradeoffs are not real.",
      "timestamp_sec": 980,
      "why": "a contestable cultural claim other speakers would push back on"
    },
    {
      "text": "But what if you just did all of it, you know? What if you forced reality to show you the tradeoff, right?",
      "timestamp_sec": 1020,
      "why": "the operational version of 'be unreasonable'"
    },
    {
      "text": "there are so many tradeoffs that you make implicitly in your head, right? Like good, fast, cheap. Now it's pick three, right?",
      "timestamp_sec": 1020,
      "why": "memorable inversion of the classic triangle"
    },
    {
      "text": "I think the only way to prove that agents work is to do the best work of our lives faster than ever before",
      "timestamp_sec": 1059,
      "why": "the talk's call to action, aimed squarely at the audience"
    },
    {
      "text": "for example, I made this deck last night in about 4 hours with Fable",
      "timestamp_sec": 1059,
